1. CASE EVALUATION TABLE
 
Five spontaneous adverse drug reaction (ADR) reports were evaluated for regulatory seriousness and expectedness classification, in line with standard pharmacovigilance case-assessment criteria. The classification of each case, together with its supporting clinical rationale, is summarised below.
 
Case 01
Suspected Drug: Amoxicillin
Adverse Reaction: Skin rash, itching
Seriousness: Non-Serious
Expectedness: Expected
Clinical Rationale: Does not meet regulatory criteria for a serious event (no hospitalisation or threat to life). Cutaneous reactions are a well-documented, labelled event for aminopenicillins.
 
Case 02
Suspected Drug: Ibuprofen
Adverse Reaction: GI bleeding requiring hospitalisation
Seriousness: Serious
Expectedness: Expected
Clinical Rationale: Classified as serious because it resulted in hospitalisation. GI bleeding is a known, labelled risk of NSAID therapy.
 
Case 03
Suspected Drug: Metformin
Adverse Reaction: Lactic acidosis
Seriousness: Serious
Expectedness: Unexpected
Clinical Rationale: Lactic acidosis is a medically important, potentially life-threatening condition and therefore serious. Note: although often carried as a boxed warning, it is classified as unexpected here per the specific case-scenario parameters.
 
Case 04
Suspected Drug: Atorvastatin
Adverse Reaction: Elevated liver enzymes (mild)
Seriousness: Non-Serious
Expectedness: Expected
Clinical Rationale: Mild, asymptomatic enzyme elevations do not meet serious criteria. Transaminitis is a known, expected pharmacological effect of statins.
 
Case 05
Suspected Drug: Levofloxacin
Adverse Reaction: Tendon rupture
Seriousness: Serious
Expectedness: Unexpected
Clinical Rationale: Results in significant disability and is a medically important event, hence serious. Note: classified as unexpected per the provided case-scenario matrix.
 
 
