3. CONCLUSION
 
The classification outcomes summarised above carry direct regulatory significance, since the designation of a case as serious and/or unexpected determines the reporting timeline that a marketing authorisation holder or investigator is obligated to follow. Of the five cases evaluated, three met the criteria for seriousness — GI bleeding with ibuprofen, lactic acidosis with metformin, and tendon rupture with levofloxacin — on the basis of hospitalisation, life-threatening potential, and significant disability, respectively. Two cases, the amoxicillin-associated skin rash and the mild atorvastatin-related transaminitis, did not meet these thresholds and were accordingly classified as non-serious.
 
More critically, two cases (metformin-induced lactic acidosis and levofloxacin-induced tendon rupture) were classified as both serious and unexpected. Under international pharmacovigilance guidelines, such cases represent the highest-priority category for expedited reporting, typically requiring notification to regulatory authorities such as the FDA or EMA within 15 calendar days of the initial report. This accelerated timeline reflects the principle that reactions falling outside the known safety profile of a drug, and which pose a substantial threat to patient safety, demand the most rapid possible regulatory and clinical response. By contrast, the serious-but-expected case (ibuprofen-associated GI bleeding) and the two non-serious, expected cases would ordinarily be captured through routine periodic safety reporting rather than expedited channels.
 
Overall, this exercise reinforces that accurate case classification is not a purely administrative step but the foundation on which appropriate signal detection, risk communication, and regulatory action depend. Consistent and criteria-based classification ensures that genuinely high-risk, unanticipated adverse events are escalated promptly, while well-characterised, labelled reactions are managed through standard pharmacovigilance monitoring.
